#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125435} :activity/id #uuid "9d6bfe73-e453-5864-9069-d3e873838966" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050065 :topic/id #uuid "2a6bebd4-c41b-566e-8d69-b14df5637cea" :topic/math-academy-id 1961} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125797 :tutorial/content "What is the matrix of the linear transformation \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nrelative to the basis\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn other words, what is the matrix $[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = [T]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}?\n$$\n\nIf you're thinking that the standard matrix \n\n\\[\nT=\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\n\\]\n\nwill work, unfortunately, it does not. For instance, consider the vector [math]\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}[/math], where we have [math][\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}[/math] and [math]\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 \\\\ -1 \\end{bmatrix}[/math]. Then:\n\\begin{align*}\n\t[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} &= \\begin{bmatrix} 9 \\\\ -5 \\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\tT\\cdot [\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}\t&= \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, we have that [math][\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\neq T \\cdot[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}[/math].\n\nIn fact, the answer is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the images of the basis vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2$ under the transformation relative to the basis:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {[T({b}_{1})]}_{B} & {[T({b}_{2})]}_{B} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis matrix is called the **matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$** or the **$\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}.$**\n\nAs a result, for the transformation $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$, with respect to the basis \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, to write ${\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}$ and ${\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) }$ with respect to our basis $\\mathcal{B},$ we assume that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the components $\\alpha_1,$ $\\alpha_2,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\alpha_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{red}\\alpha_2}\\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}\\quad \\text{and}\\quad {\\color{blue}\\beta_1}\\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}\\beta_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSolving these two systems gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}16 & 25 \\\\ -9 & -14\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "065ca75d-4b23-587e-9942-33e8dce0545f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3583 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a3b5f073-2339-52af-9f1e-fd0da19753c1" :step/math-academy-id 20139 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125437}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125436 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124539 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124348 :question/id #uuid "9f96c35d-28bb-533d-98d0-8608b791c8e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5513"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394387}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Two-Dimensional Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "0529eb7d-ff67-5070-9cd6-46a16efbb17a" :step/math-academy-id 20140 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125439}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125437 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124540 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124349 :question/id #uuid "a2b2d84f-cf3b-52a7-98e0-e659d04697f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5514"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "823a7d28-5a92-5901-8ee0-e5116df6766b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394380}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Image of a Vector Given the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis"} :step/id #uuid "e58972de-290c-5656-9ece-acf4cc305d31" :step/math-academy-id 20141 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125438}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125798 :tutorial/content "In general, consider a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\dots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V$ and a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow V$. For any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+\\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{b}_n\n$$\n\nor, equivalently $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]$.\nThen the matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ [T({b}_{1})]_{B} & [T({b}_{2})]_{B} & ⋯ & [T({b}_{n})]_{B} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ [T({b}_{1})]_{B} & [T({b}_{2})]_{B} & ⋯ & [T({b}_{n})]_{B} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c40a1e69-05eb-58c1-81f9-0a459daca5ff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3584 :tutorial/title "The General Case"} :step/id #uuid "c5270756-13af-5caa-bba0-3b34772bfcb9" :step/math-academy-id 20142 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125496}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125439 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124541 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124350 :question/id #uuid "d46f548c-c15c-5716-9f37-7f9dfa751f35" :question/math-academy-id "e-5515"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366420}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Three-Dimensional Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "520c428b-7a04-5d55-84b6-a53202f37188" :step/math-academy-id 20143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124576 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124383 :question/id #uuid "71ac5a84-d8e8-5de6-8adc-e0172457a772" :question/math-academy-id "e-7249"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392252}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "d481fcd2-020b-57b6-9aa3-71fe984a8025" :step/math-academy-id 26810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125436}}] :activity/title "The Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124746}]}
